TA_EXCERTO = "\"Contrary to what might seem to the reader in the beginning, nothing in my words insinuates the nonexistence of deliberate reflection or the denial of the difference between one type of cognition and the other. This difference is not denied but reformulated. Above all, this difference is still “animal”—an extension, or complexification, of animality. While it is also more than that, or enables more than that—which also needs to be explained—what has been mostly ignored so far is not the rationality of this difference but the irrationality of this difference—which, nevertheless, has been present in our intuitions and narratives. Take, according to the Judaeo-Christian myths, the first memorable act by the first being who was born fully human: the murder of Abel by Cain. This was an act that, being more than animal, is founded in animality. Murder, taking a life, not for another instinctive need (like eating), but as an end in itself, is a uniquely human capacity, made possible not only by the consciousness of death but by the capacity for thought. As Massimo Recalcati says, “Violence does not erupt because man turns into a beast. In fact, the temptation to commit violence doesn't exist in the animal world” (2022: 58). To kill his brother, the world of instincts, emotions, and motivations had to become human. But this becoming human is not based on the existence of a cartesian Self or a higher system that controls, inhibits, and educates instincts; rather, it is based on a complexification of these instincts that can take various forms: Cain had to think about killing Abel. He had to boil in anger, envy, and repeatedly resent how his parents now looked at his younger brother. He had to feel jealousy, not only in Abel’s presence, but in his absence. He had to imagine the act before carrying it out. And all of this is uniquely human, all of this is thinking. Therefore, all of this must be taken into account when we aim to uncover what makes us thinking beings.\" — in Introduction."

